---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { mailto, SITE } from '../data/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="KubeHeads is a small engineering practice. You talk to the people who write the code, because there is nobody else to talk to."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Small on purpose.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        KubeHeads is an engineering practice, not a body shop. You talk to the people who write
        the code, because there is nobody else to talk to.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--sunken">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <h2>What that means in practice</h2>
      <p>
        There is no account manager translating your problem into a statement of work and then
        translating it back. There is no bench of juniors quietly assigned to your project after
        the pitch. The engineer in the first call is the engineer who ships it.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also means we take on fewer projects than we could. We would rather do three things
        properly this quarter than nine badly. If we are not the right fit, we will say so early
        and point you somewhere better.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.5rem">Why the three disciplines go together</h2>
      <p>
        AI automation, optimization, and engineering are usually sold by three different firms.
        That split is where the work dies: the model vendor waits on the data team, the data team
        waits on whoever owns deployment, and the project quietly becomes nobody's problem.
      </p>
      <p>
        Covering all three is not a claim to be world-class at each in isolation. It is a claim
        that the seams between them are where projects fail, and that removing the seams is worth
        more to you than a marginally better specialist on any one of them.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.5rem">Where we are</h2>
      <p>
        Budapest, Hungary — working with clients across the EU, remote-first, comfortable in
        English and Hungarian.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.5rem">The honest status</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        We are early, and we would rather tell you that plainly than pad a client logo wall. If
        that is a dealbreaker, entirely fair. If it means you get senior attention at a stage
        where most firms would hand you a junior — that is the trade.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href={mailto()}>{SITE.email}</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/approach">How we work</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .narrow { max-width: 68ch; }
  .narrow h2 { font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 2.6vw, 1.9rem); }
</style>
